#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# dev/stabilize-guard — THE WRITE GUARD OF A STABILIZATION RUN: a PreToolUse hook that
# keeps every agent of a run that is not one of its writers from writing the run's
# repository (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-16, "The write guard of a stabilization run";
# implementation/stabilization-workflow.md -> The write guard).
#
# WHY. In the staged canary's third root, part 2 (2026-10-16), a driver of a review row
# ran an ad-hoc probe whose `R` a shell glob error had left empty, so its commands ran in
# the canary's clone — the run's repository — and it committed there and ran `git reset
# --hard`. Nothing in the tools prevented it, and nothing but a stray commit is seen
# afterwards: not a write to the working tree, the index, `.git/config` or `.git/hooks`.
# In the real run that repository is the human's main working tree. The human ruled that
# the guard PREVENTS, and that the runs stay in bypassPermissions: a hook's `deny` holds
# in that mode, and an allow rule does not (implementation/stabilization-workflow.md ->
# The write guard, where the documentation it rests on is cited).
#
# WHERE IT BINDS — INSIDE A RUN, AND NOWHERE ELSE. The repository is the git work tree of
# the session's project (CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, which Claude Code sets for every hook). The
# guard binds where that tree has checked out a run's loop branch or one of its round
# branches — `fix/<run>`, `fix/<run>-r<N>`, `fix/<run>-r<N>-part<M>`, the harness's
# `branchName` — AND completions/artifacts/<run>/opening.md is a file there: a stage runs
# only with the loop branch checked out, and a run is opened when that file is there
# (dev/stabilize-record: A RUN IS OPENED). On any other branch, a detached HEAD, or a
# project that is no git repository, it says nothing at all: a milestone build, a fix
# branch with no run and an ad-hoc session are as they were.
#
# WHO IS FENCED — EVERY SUBAGENT BUT THE RUN'S WRITERS (and, while a runtime probe is
# active, every subagent: below). The hook input's `agent_type`
# names the subagent a tool call is made in. Absent or empty, the call is the session's
# own — the human's, or the orchestrator's — and is never fenced. `build-git` (the git
# steps: their acts write the run's record, commit and push it) and `build-fixer` (a
# fixer commits its fix on the round branch) are the WRITERS, and are never fenced. EVERY
# OTHER TYPE IS, known or not: the hook cannot tell who launched an agent, so a type
# nobody classified is fenced and never let through. tooling-tests/dev_stabilize_guard.rs
# holds the writers to the types the harness launches, and the fenced types the harness
# launches to a list a change has to name.
#
# WHILE A RUNTIME PROBE IS ACTIVE — ANYWHERE, AND FOR EVERY SUBAGENT (the plan before the
# opening's task T-NR; DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-29, "The build session runs the single T-NR
# runtime probe", condition 5: "no push" and "no commit" are held by a mechanism). The
# `neutralized` probe's first act writes a MARKER, `stabilize-probe-active`, in the COMMON git
# directory of the repository (`git rev-parse --git-common-dir`), naming the probe and its
# scratch root; its verdict removes it (dev/stabilize-probe). While that file stands, the
# guard binds on any branch, inside a run or outside one, and fences EVERY subagent — the
# writers too, `build-git` and `build-fixer` — as an ALLOWLIST:
#
#   Bash            ONE plain invocation of `dev/stabilize-probe` or `dev/stabilize-record`,
#                   in the grammar below, from the repository's root, runs; the same call
#                   from another directory is refused (`directory`); every other command —
#                   a push, a commit, `git -c x=y push`, `env git push`, a script written
#                   and then run, a call of the step tool — is REFUSED (`probe`). Nothing
#                   is run under the fence: there is nothing else to run.
#   Write · Edit ·  a target under the probe's scratch root runs; any other is REFUSED
#   NotebookEdit    (`probe`), judged on the file system as below.
#   Monitor ·       REFUSED (`probe`): a shell the list cannot hold.
#   PowerShell
#   Agent · Task ·  REFUSED (`probe`), whatever it would launch: the probe's set of agents is
#   Workflow        fixed and counted (one subject, three step agents), so no subagent grows
#                   it. The probe's own four are launched by the harness — the runtime's
#                   `agent()` calls under the main session's `Workflow` call — and never by
#                   a subagent's tool call, so this refusal cannot block them. The settings'
#                   matcher, below, hands the hook all three; a `Task` or a `Workflow` call
#                   outside a probe, and the session's own, it answers with nothing.
#
# The session's own calls are never fenced, under a probe as everywhere: the main session
# removes the marker of a probe that was cut (the plan before the opening, §1: the guard
# refuses commits and pushes "from every subagent"). A marker whose scratch root is gone is STALE, and one that is no JSON object
# naming a scratch root CANNOT BE READ: each binds all the same — nothing is written
# anywhere — and every refusal says so, until the main session removes the file.
# WHY THE COMMON GIT DIRECTORY, AND WHY THAT IS SAFE. It is no tracked path, and git never
# reads a file of that name; and it is the one place every worktree of the repository
# shares, so a session in the loop checkout or in a lane's worktree is bound alike. THAT
# SHARING IS HARMLESS ONLY BECAUSE THE PROBE IS RUN WITH NO OTHER AGENT OF THE SESSION IN
# FLIGHT (the plan before the opening, §1): while the marker stands, a builder in any lane's
# worktree cannot commit, push or gate.
#
# WHAT A FENCED AGENT IS REFUSED, OR HOW ITS COMMAND RUNS:
#
#   Write · Edit ·  a target that resolves under the repository's work tree or its git
#   NotebookEdit    directories is REFUSED (`repository`). Resolved on the file system:
#                   through links, `..`, a `/var` that is `/private/var`, another case
#                   of a name on a volume that ignores case — every existing ancestor is
#                   compared by its device and inode, never by its spelling. A new file
#                   whose parent is not there yet is judged by the nearest ancestor that
#                   is. Anywhere else, the guard says nothing.
#   Bash            ONE PLAIN INVOCATION of `dev/stabilize-step`, `dev/stabilize-record`
#                   or `dev/stabilize-probe` — the grammar of the allow rules and of the
#                   harness's prompts: the tool's path as its first word, then plain
#                   words, bare (A-Z a-z 0-9 and _ . / : = @ % + , -) or in single quotes
#                   (those and the space and parentheses), and nothing else on the line —
#                   from the repository's root, is let through UNFENCED: those tools are
#                   how a run's record is written. The same call from another directory
#                   is refused (`directory`). A command that calls one of the three in
#                   any other shape — after a `cd` and `&&`, before a `;` or an `echo`,
#                   by an absolute or a `./` path, through an interpreter, with a pipe
#                   or a redirect — is REFUSED (`shape`), with the shape it is taken in.
#                   EVERY OTHER COMMAND RUNS UNDER THE WRITE FENCE: it is rewritten,
#                   before it runs, into ONE invocation of dev/stabilize-fence, which runs
#                   it unchanged under the operating system's write fence — nothing under
#                   the repository's work tree or its git directories can be written by
#                   it or by anything it starts — and says so on stderr. Where the fence
#                   cannot be built (no macOS sandbox, no fence tool beside this one) the
#                   command is REFUSED (`no-fence`), never run open. The rewrite is
#                   allowed in bypassPermissions, where nothing is asked anyway; in any
#                   other mode it is ASKED, with the fenced command shown — an `allow`
#                   would approve a command the mode would have asked about.
#   Monitor ·       a shell this guard cannot fence: REFUSED (`shell`).
#   PowerShell
#   Agent · Task    a fenced agent may not launch a writer: REFUSED (`writer`).
#   Workflow        a fenced agent may start no workflow, which can launch anything, a
#                   writer among it: REFUSED (`writer`).
#
# FAIL CLOSED. For a fenced type, what the guard cannot read — no CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, a
# git that does not answer, a tool input without its path or its command — is a refusal
# (`unknown`), never a pass. An input that is no JSON object exits 2 with the reason on
# stderr, which Claude Code takes as a block. And the settings run this hook with
# `onFailure: block`, so a hook that cannot start blocks too.
#
# Usage:
#
#   dev/stabilize-guard            a PreToolUse hook: the event's JSON on stdin. Prints
#                                  nothing where it has no decision, and otherwise ONE
#                                  JSON object, {hookSpecificOutput: {hookEventName:
#                                  PreToolUse, permissionDecision: deny | allow | ask,
#                                  permissionDecisionReason [, updatedInput]}}, and exits 0.
#                                  A refusal's reason is one line that opens with
#                                  `stabilize-guard: refused <word>: `.
#   dev/stabilize-guard --help     this header.
#
# The settings that run it — `.claude/settings.json`, one entry under hooks.PreToolUse:
#
#   {"matcher": "Bash|Write|Edit|NotebookEdit|Monitor|PowerShell|Agent|Task|Workflow",
#    "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": "${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}/dev/stabilize-guard",
#               "args": [], "timeout": 30, "onFailure": "block"}]}
#
# — exec form (`args`), so that a project path with a space in it is one word.
#
# STABILIZE_FENCE_SANDBOX names the sandbox program instead of /usr/bin/sandbox-exec —
# for this tool's suite, which asks what happens where there is none; dev/stabilize-fence
# reads the same name.
#
# Needs python3 and git. It writes nothing.

import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys

PROG = "stabilize-guard"
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
FENCE = os.path.join(HERE, "stabilize-fence")
SANDBOX = os.environ.get("STABILIZE_FENCE_SANDBOX") or "/usr/bin/sandbox-exec"

# The run's writers: the agent types whose job is to write the run's repository.
WRITERS = ("build-git", "build-fixer")
# The three tools a run's record is written through, by their path from the repository's
# root — the allow rules' and the harness's prompts' (PROMPT_TOOLS of the fence suite).
TOOLS = ("dev/stabilize-step", "dev/stabilize-record", "dev/stabilize-probe")
# While a runtime probe is active: its marker, by its name in the common git directory
# (dev/stabilize-probe's MARKER), and the two tools a subagent may call — the probe's own,
# and the record script its record writer's act runs a copy of — and the tools that launch
# an agent or a workflow, which no subagent may call while it stands.
MARKER = "stabilize-probe-active"
PROBE_TOOLS = ("dev/stabilize-probe", "dev/stabilize-record")
LAUNCHERS = ("Agent", "Task", "Workflow")
# Where a run lies, and how its branches are named (the harness's RUNS_ROOT, branchName).
RUNS = "completions/artifacts"
LOOP = re.compile(r"fix/([a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*)")
ROUND = re.compile(r"(.+)-r[1-9][0-9]*(?:-part[1-9][0-9]*)?")
# One word of a plain invocation: bare, or in single quotes with nothing a shell reads.
BARE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9_./:=@%+,-]+")
QUOTED = re.compile(r"'[A-Za-z0-9_./:=@%+, ()-]*'")
# A call of one of the three tools at the place of a command: the line's start, after an
# operator, a parenthesis, a brace or a backquote, or after a word that runs the next one —
# with any path before the tool's own and any assignments before that.
CALLED = re.compile(r"(?:^|[;&|(){}`\n]|\$\(|(?:^|(?<=[\s;&|(){}`]))(?:then|do|else|exec|command|env|nohup|time|xargs|sudo|python3?|sh|bash|zsh)(?=\s))\s*(?:[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=\S*\s+)*\S*?(dev/stabilize-(?:step|record|probe))(?![A-Za-z0-9_.-])")
# The tools whose input names a file to write, and the field that names it.
FILE_TOOLS = {"Write": "file_path", "Edit": "file_path", "NotebookEdit": "notebook_path"}
SHELLS = ("Monitor", "PowerShell")


def decide(decision, reason=None, updated=None):
    out = {"hookEventName": "PreToolUse", "permissionDecision": decision}
    if reason is not None:
        out["permissionDecisionReason"] = reason
    if updated is not None:
        out["updatedInput"] = updated
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({"hookSpecificOutput": out}) + "\n")
    sys.exit(0)


def refused(word, detail):
    decide("deny", "%s: refused %s: %s" % (PROG, word, detail))


def git_env():
    env = {name: value for name, value in os.environ.items() if not name.startswith("GIT_")}
    env["GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS"] = "0"
    return env


def git(at, *args):
    """What git prints, stripped — or None where it does not answer."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", at] + list(args), env=git_env(), stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, universal_newlines=True, timeout=20)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        raise LookupError("git did not answer in %s" % at)
    return done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else None


def resolved(at, path):
    """A path, made absolute against `at` and resolved through every link it has."""
    return os.path.realpath(os.path.join(at, path))


def nearest(path):
    """The nearest ancestor of `path`, itself included, that is there."""
    while not os.path.lexists(path):
        parent = os.path.dirname(path)
        if parent == path:
            break
        path = parent
    return path


def identity(path):
    try:
        found = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return (found.st_dev, found.st_ino)


def under(path, places):
    """Whether `path` — resolved, with what is not there yet judged by its nearest
    ancestor that is — lies at or below one of `places`, compared by device and inode."""
    marks = {identity(place) for place in places} - {None}
    at = nearest(os.path.realpath(path))
    while True:
        if identity(at) in marks:
            return True
        parent = os.path.dirname(at)
        if parent == at:
            return False
        at = parent


def the_run(project):
    """(the work tree, its git directories, the run) where the guard binds — or None."""
    top = git(project, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if top is None:
        return None
    top = os.path.realpath(top)
    branch = git(top, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "--short", "HEAD")
    named = LOOP.fullmatch(branch or "")
    if not named:
        return None
    slug = named.group(1)
    candidates = [slug]
    stripped = ROUND.fullmatch(slug)
    if stripped:
        candidates.append(stripped.group(1))
    run = next((name for name in candidates if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(top, RUNS, name, "opening.md"))), None)
    if run is None:
        return None
    dirs = git(top, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir", "--git-dir")
    if dirs is None:
        raise LookupError("git does not name the git directories of %s" % top)
    places = [top] + [os.path.realpath(line) for line in dirs.splitlines() if line]
    return top, places, run


def words(command):
    """The words of a command line, split at spaces outside single quotes — or None where
    a quote is left open."""
    found, word, quoted = [], "", False
    for char in command:
        if char == "'":
            quoted = not quoted
        if char == " " and not quoted:
            if word:
                found.append(word)
            word = ""
        else:
            word += char
    if quoted:
        return None
    if word:
        found.append(word)
    return found


def plain_call(command, tools=TOOLS):
    """Whether a command is ONE plain invocation of one of `tools` — the three tools, unless
    a probe is active."""
    said = words(command.strip(" \n"))
    return bool(said) and said[0] in tools and all(BARE.fullmatch(word) or QUOTED.fullmatch(word) for word in said[1:])


def the_probe(project):
    """(the work tree, the marker's path, the scratch root it names or None, what is wrong
    with it or None) where a probe's marker stands — or None."""
    top = git(project, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if top is None:
        return None
    top = os.path.realpath(top)
    common = git(top, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
    if common is None:
        raise LookupError("git does not name the common git directory of %s" % top)
    marker = os.path.join(os.path.realpath(common), MARKER)
    if not os.path.lexists(marker):
        return None
    try:
        with open(marker, encoding="utf-8") as file:
            said = json.load(file)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as why:
        return top, marker, None, "it cannot be read (%s)" % " ".join(str(why).split())
    scratch = said.get("scratch") if isinstance(said, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(scratch, str) or not os.path.isabs(scratch):
        return top, marker, None, "it cannot be read: it names no scratch root"
    if not os.path.isdir(scratch):
        return top, marker, scratch, "it is stale: the scratch root it names, %s, is gone, so nothing may be written anywhere" % scratch
    return top, marker, scratch, None


def under_a_probe(agent, tool, given, event, probe):
    """The decision on a subagent's call while a runtime probe is active: an ALLOWLIST."""
    top, marker, scratch, wrong = probe

    def refused_probe(what):
        state = (" and " + wrong) if wrong else ""
        refused("probe", "%s may not %s while a runtime probe is active — its marker %s stands%s. While it stands every subagent of this session, a writer too, runs only ONE plain call of %s, typed alone from the repository's root %s, and writes only under the probe's scratch root%s; the probe's verdict removes the marker, and the main session removes the marker of a probe that was cut" % (agent, what, marker, state, " or ".join(PROBE_TOOLS), top, " " + scratch if scratch else ""))

    if tool in FILE_TOOLS:
        path = given.get(FILE_TOOLS[tool])
        if not isinstance(path, str) or not path:
            refused("unknown", "the %s call names no file (%s), so the guard cannot tell where it writes" % (tool, FILE_TOOLS[tool]))
        if wrong is None and under(resolved(event.get("cwd") or top, path), [scratch]):
            return
        refused_probe("write %s" % path)
    if tool in SHELLS:
        refused_probe("use %s" % tool)
    if tool in LAUNCHERS:
        refused_probe("launch an agent or a workflow by %s: the probe's set of agents is fixed and counted, and the harness alone launches it" % tool)
    if tool != "Bash":
        return
    command = given.get("command")
    if not isinstance(command, str) or not command.strip():
        refused("unknown", "the Bash call names no command, so the guard cannot tell what it runs")
    if plain_call(command, PROBE_TOOLS):
        here = event.get("cwd")
        if isinstance(here, str) and here and identity(resolved(top, here)) == identity(top):
            return
        refused("directory", "a call of %s runs from the repository's root, %s, and this one was made from %s: run the same line again from there" % (words(command.strip(" \n"))[0], top, here))
    refused_probe("run this command")


def fence_missing():
    """Why the write fence cannot be built here, or None where it can."""
    if sys.platform != "darwin":
        return "this host is no macOS (%s), and the write fence is macOS's sandbox: no fence is built for another" % sys.platform
    if not os.access(SANDBOX, os.X_OK):
        return "the sandbox program %s is not there" % SANDBOX
    if not os.access(FENCE, os.X_OK):
        return "the fence tool %s is not there" % FENCE
    return None


def main():
    if sys.argv[1:] in (["--help"], ["-h"]):
        with open(os.path.realpath(__file__), encoding="utf-8") as source:
            for line in source.read().splitlines()[1:]:
                if not line.startswith("#"):
                    break
                sys.stdout.write(line[2:] + "\n")
        return
    if sys.argv[1:]:
        sys.stderr.write("%s: refused usage: it takes no argument but --help; a PreToolUse event is read on stdin\n" % PROG)
        sys.exit(2)
    try:
        event = json.loads(sys.stdin.read())
    except ValueError:
        event = None
    if not isinstance(event, dict):
        sys.stderr.write("%s: refused unknown: the hook's input is no JSON object, so nothing says whose call this is — it is blocked\n" % PROG)
        sys.exit(2)
    agent = event.get("agent_type")
    if not agent:
        return
    tool = event.get("tool_name")
    given = event.get("tool_input")
    given = given if isinstance(given, dict) else {}
    project = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR")
    if project:
        try:
            probe = the_probe(project)
        except LookupError as why:
            if agent in WRITERS:
                return
            refused("unknown", "%s: a call of a fenced agent is refused where the guard cannot tell what it would write" % why)
        if probe is not None:
            under_a_probe(agent, tool, given, event, probe)
            return
    if agent in WRITERS:
        return
    if not project:
        refused("unknown", "CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR is not set, so the guard cannot tell whether %s works inside a stabilization run: a call of a fenced agent is refused where that cannot be told" % agent)
    try:
        bound = the_run(project)
    except LookupError as why:
        refused("unknown", "%s: a call of a fenced agent is refused where the guard cannot tell what it would write" % why)
    if bound is None:
        return
    top, places, run = bound
    if tool in FILE_TOOLS:
        path = given.get(FILE_TOOLS[tool])
        if not isinstance(path, str) or not path:
            refused("unknown", "the %s call names no file (%s), so the guard cannot tell where it writes" % (tool, FILE_TOOLS[tool]))
        if under(resolved(event.get("cwd") or top, path), places):
            refused("repository", "%s may not write %s: inside the stabilization run `%s` no agent but a writer (%s) writes the run's repository %s or its git directory. Write under your scratch root, and hand a report or a filing to dev/stabilize-record by the one plain call your prompt spells" % (agent, path, run, ", ".join(WRITERS), top))
        return
    if tool in SHELLS:
        refused("shell", "%s may not use %s inside the stabilization run `%s`: it runs a shell the write guard cannot fence. Use Bash, whose commands run under the write fence" % (agent, tool, run))
    if tool in ("Agent", "Task"):
        if given.get("subagent_type") in WRITERS:
            refused("writer", "%s may not launch a %s inside the stabilization run `%s`: a writer is launched by the harness alone" % (agent, given.get("subagent_type"), run))
        return
    if tool == "Workflow":
        refused("writer", "%s may not start a workflow by Workflow inside the stabilization run `%s`: a workflow can launch any agent, a writer among them, and a writer is launched by the harness alone" % (agent, run))
    if tool != "Bash":
        return
    command = given.get("command")
    if not isinstance(command, str) or not command.strip():
        refused("unknown", "the Bash call names no command, so the guard cannot tell what it runs")
    if plain_call(command):
        here = event.get("cwd")
        if isinstance(here, str) and here and identity(resolved(top, here)) == identity(top):
            return
        refused("directory", "a call of %s runs from the repository's root, %s, and this one was made from %s: run the same line again from there" % (words(command.strip(" \n"))[0], top, here))
    called = CALLED.search(command)
    if called:
        tool_path = called.group(1)
        refused("shape", "a call of %s is taken only as ONE plain invocation, typed alone: `%s <its words>`, the tool's path first and its words after it, exactly as your prompt spells the call — no `cd`, no `&&` or `;` and no second command, no `echo`, no pipe and no redirect, no `$`, and no path before `dev/`. Its exit status is the call's own. Type the call again in that shape" % (tool_path, tool_path))
    missing = fence_missing()
    if missing:
        refused("no-fence", "%s — inside the stabilization run `%s` every command of %s runs under the write fence, and none is run open" % (missing, run, agent))
    updated = dict(given)
    updated["command"] = "%s --repo %s -- %s" % (shlex.quote(FENCE), shlex.quote(top), shlex.quote(command))
    if event.get("permission_mode") == "bypassPermissions":
        decide("allow", "%s: the command runs under the write fence of the stabilization run `%s`" % (PROG, run), updated)
    decide("ask", "%s: inside the stabilization run `%s` this command of %s runs only under the write fence — the command shown is that fenced command" % (PROG, run, agent), updated)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
